{"context":"walkie's devices share a tailnet, which already provides NAT traversal and its own DERP relay fallback. That raises the question of what walkie's own server should carry. Three topologies were on the table: route all traffic through the server; keep the server to coordination only and send all data peer-to-peer; or attempt direct and fall back to relaying through the server. The choice determines server bandwidth, latency, failure behaviour during a call, and the scope of application-level encryption.","decision":"**Hybrid.** The coordinator always owns the control plane. The data plane attempts a direct peer-to-peer path over the tailnet and falls back to relaying through the coordinator when a direct path cannot be established.\n\n**The fallback is implemented in stage 2, not in the MVP.** In the MVP every byte already passes through the coordinator — text is control-plane traffic, and voice notes are attachments — so there is no direct path to fall back from. Building the fallback before the direct path exists would be building the exception before the rule. The topology is decided now because it constrains the wire protocol and the security model; only its implementation is deferred.","consequences":"**Positive.** Live calls survive a coordinator restart once the direct path is in place. Server bandwidth for direct calls is zero. Latency on the common path is the minimum the network allows. And there is a defined recourse when a direct path genuinely cannot form — a device with a degraded Tailscale daemon, or an ACL that permits reaching the coordinator but not a peer.\n\n**Negative, and accepted deliberately.**\n\n1. **Two data paths, so roughly twice the test surface.** Both must be exercised, including the transition between them. This is the real cost of the choice.\n2. **Partial overlap with Tailscale's own DERP fallback.** Tailscale already relays when it cannot go direct, so walkie's fallback covers a narrower residual case than it first appears to. It is justified only for failures below the tailnet — not as a substitute for DERP.\n3. **Relayed payloads leave the WireGuard tunnel at the coordinator.** This is what pulls application-level encryption into scope for the relay path, and it is a direct cost of choosing hybrid over pure peer-to-peer.\n4. **The fallback can mask a broken tailnet.** If direct paths never form, a hybrid design keeps working while quietly relaying everything. The direct-versus-relay ratio is therefore a required metric, not an optional one — without it this decision is unobservable in production.","alternativesConsidered":"**Full relay through the server.** Simplest client, one connection, easy central audit. Rejected: doubles server bandwidth per voice stream, adds a hop to every audio frame, and makes the coordinator a single point of failure for conversations in progress.\n\n**Coordinator-only, pure peer-to-peer data plane.** Lowest latency, least code, and the smallest encryption scope — queued messages would be the only coordinator blind spot. Rejected because it leaves no recourse when a direct path cannot form; on a fleet that includes embedded devices and locations with constrained links, 'it should work' is not a recovery plan.\n\n**Relying solely on Tailscale DERP as the fallback.** Attractive, and close to what the hybrid actually does in most cases. Rejected as insufficient on its own because it does not cover failures of the local Tailscale daemon, which is precisely the case where an emergency-communication tool most needs a path."}